1
00:00:01,645 --> 00:00:03,499
Look up at the night sky.

2
00:00:03,799 --> 00:00:07,102
Near the southern horizon, a
bright star shines.

3
00:00:07,402 --> 00:00:10,368
It looks calm. It looks close. It is not.

4
00:00:10,668 --> 00:00:15,766
That point of light is Alpha Centauri, the
nearest star system to our Sun.

5
00:00:16,066 --> 00:00:20,953
And the journey to reach it may be the most
terrifying voyage humanity has

6
00:00:20,953 --> 00:00:21,877
ever imagined.

7
00:00:22,177 --> 00:00:24,926
In the next eight minutes, we
will fly there.

8
00:00:25,226 --> 00:00:30,519
We will cross 40 trillion kilometres of
darkness, and meet the dangers waiting

9
00:00:30,519 --> 00:00:31,333
in the void.

10
00:00:31,633 --> 00:00:36,383
Stay until the end, because the last part
changes how you see the stars.

11
00:00:39,710 --> 00:00:44,209
Let's start with the farthest explorer we
have ever sent toward the stars.

12
00:00:44,509 --> 00:00:52,612
Voyager 1 launched in 1977, and entered
interstellar space in 2012.

13
00:00:52,912 --> 00:00:58,423
It travels at about seventeen kilometres
every second, many times faster than

14
00:00:58,423 --> 00:00:59,497
a rifle bullet.

15
00:00:59,797 --> 00:01:04,628
Yet if it were aimed at Alpha Centauri, it
would need around seventy-five

16
00:01:04,628 --> 00:01:06,283
thousand years to arrive.

17
00:01:06,583 --> 00:01:12,632
Seventy-five thousand years ago, no city, no
farm, and no written word existed

18
00:01:12,632 --> 00:01:14,028
anywhere on Earth.

19
00:01:14,328 --> 00:01:19,589
Even the Parker Solar Probe, the fastest
machine ever built, would take nearly

20
00:01:19,589 --> 00:01:21,005
seven thousand years.

21
00:01:24,332 --> 00:01:26,274
So how far is it, really?

22
00:01:26,574 --> 00:01:31,285
Alpha Centauri lies about 4.37
light years away.

23
00:01:31,585 --> 00:01:36,897
Light, the fastest thing in the universe, needs
more than four years to make this trip.

24
00:01:37,197 --> 00:01:40,540
In kilometres, that is roughly
forty trillion.

25
00:01:40,840 --> 00:01:45,826
Or, in units our Solar System understands,
about two hundred and seventy

26
00:01:45,826 --> 00:01:49,565
thousand times the distance from
the Earth to the Sun.

27
00:01:49,865 --> 00:01:54,723
Shrink the Sun to a single grain of sand,
and the next star would still be

28
00:01:54,723 --> 00:01:56,692
nearly thirty kilometres away.

29
00:02:00,019 --> 00:02:02,960
And what waits there? Not one
star, but three.

30
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:09,051
Alpha Centauri A and B are sun-like stars
that circle each other once every

31
00:02:09,051 --> 00:02:13,451
eighty years, roughly as far apart
as the Sun and Uranus.

32
00:02:13,751 --> 00:02:19,232
Far beyond them, about a fifth of a light
year away, a small red dwarf called

33
00:02:19,232 --> 00:02:21,367
Proxima Centauri drifts along.

34
00:02:21,667 --> 00:02:26,907
At 4.24 light years, Proxima is the
closest star to us of all.

35
00:02:27,207 --> 00:02:31,422
Yet it is so dim that you can never
see it without a telescope.

36
00:02:34,749 --> 00:02:39,862
Around that little star circles a world
astronomers cannot stop thinking about.

37
00:02:40,162 --> 00:02:45,914
Proxima b, discovered in 2016, is a rocky
planet with roughly the mass of

38
00:02:45,914 --> 00:02:51,271
Earth, sitting in the habitable zone
where liquid water could exist.

39
00:02:51,571 --> 00:02:56,687
But look closer. It orbits its star every
eleven days, about eight times

40
00:02:56,687 --> 00:02:59,102
closer than Mercury is to our Sun.

41
00:02:59,402 --> 00:03:05,994
And its star is violent. In March 2017, a
single flare made Proxima a thousand

42
00:03:05,994 --> 00:03:10,303
times brighter in radio light,
in just ten seconds.

43
00:03:10,603 --> 00:03:15,355
Flares like that could strip away an
atmosphere, and sterilise a surface.

44
00:03:15,655 --> 00:03:21,902
And around Alpha Centauri A, the James Webb
telescope spotted a faint dot in

45
00:03:21,902 --> 00:03:28,067
2024: a possible giant planet, about twice
as far from its star as Earth is

46
00:03:28,067 --> 00:03:29,135
from the Sun.

47
00:03:29,435 --> 00:03:35,255
It was seen only once. For now, it remains a
candidate, not a confirmed world.

48
00:03:38,582 --> 00:03:42,114
So the destination is real. The
problem is speed.

49
00:03:42,414 --> 00:03:47,047
To arrive within a human lifetime, we
would need to travel at a serious

50
00:03:47,047 --> 00:03:49,070
fraction of the speed of light.

51
00:03:49,370 --> 00:03:54,300
At ten percent of light speed, the trip
takes about forty-four years. At

52
00:03:54,300 --> 00:03:56,559
twenty percent, about twenty-two.

53
00:03:56,859 --> 00:04:01,542
But rockets that carry their own fuel
cannot come close to those speeds.

54
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:07,431
So engineers asked a radical question: what if
the ship carried no fuel at all?

55
00:04:10,759 --> 00:04:16,319
In 2016, Yuri Milner announced, alongside
Stephen Hawking, Breakthrough

56
00:04:16,319 --> 00:04:20,391
Starshot, a hundred million
dollar research program.

57
00:04:20,691 --> 00:04:26,408
The idea: build the most powerful laser array
ever made, around a hundred gigawatts.

58
00:04:26,708 --> 00:04:31,761
Then use it to push a fleet of tiny probes,
each a chip weighing about a gram,

59
00:04:31,761 --> 00:04:33,770
attached to a mirror-like sail.

60
00:04:34,070 --> 00:04:38,818
In just minutes, the laser would blast them
up to twenty percent of the speed

61
00:04:38,818 --> 00:04:42,518
of light, more than two hundred
million kilometres per hour.

62
00:04:42,818 --> 00:04:46,519
They would reach Alpha Centauri in
about twenty years.

63
00:04:46,819 --> 00:04:50,977
And their pictures would need another
four years to travel home.

64
00:04:54,304 --> 00:04:57,461
But the space between the
stars is not empty.

65
00:04:57,761 --> 00:05:01,544
It is filled with thin gas, and
tiny grains of dust.

66
00:05:01,844 --> 00:05:07,019
At normal speeds, they are harmless. At
twenty percent of light speed, every

67
00:05:07,019 --> 00:05:08,586
grain becomes a bullet.

68
00:05:08,886 --> 00:05:14,132
A single sail could be struck by around a
billion dust particles on the way.

69
00:05:14,432 --> 00:05:20,508
And a grain just one millimetre across would
hit with the energy of dozens of

70
00:05:20,508 --> 00:05:21,850
kilograms of TNT.

71
00:05:22,150 --> 00:05:27,533
A big ship could be destroyed in an instant.
For the tiny sails, the plan is a

72
00:05:27,533 --> 00:05:31,259
numbers game: launch thousands,
and hope some survive.

73
00:05:34,587 --> 00:05:37,529
There is a second killer, and
you cannot see it.

74
00:05:37,829 --> 00:05:43,570
Space is threaded with cosmic rays, fast
charged particles that raise the risk

75
00:05:43,570 --> 00:05:45,999
of cancer even on short missions.

76
00:05:46,299 --> 00:05:51,836
At relativistic speed, it gets worse: even a
single hydrogen atom slams in so

77
00:05:51,836 --> 00:05:56,725
hard that, from the ship's point of view,
it acts like a cosmic ray.

78
00:05:57,025 --> 00:06:02,218
Analyses of fast starships find that a crew
at very high speed would need a

79
00:06:02,218 --> 00:06:07,064
shield several metres thick, just to
survive the gas it flies through.

80
00:06:10,391 --> 00:06:15,948
Now imagine the impossible: a crewed ship
that accelerates at one g, the same

81
00:06:15,948 --> 00:06:18,257
pull you feel standing on Earth.

82
00:06:18,557 --> 00:06:21,385
Halfway there, it flips
around, and brakes.

83
00:06:21,685 --> 00:06:25,952
No engine we know could do this. But
the physics is clear.

84
00:06:26,252 --> 00:06:31,628
The crew would age about three and a half years,
while about six years pass on Earth.

85
00:06:31,928 --> 00:06:36,877
At the midpoint, they would be moving at
ninety-five percent of the speed of light.

86
00:06:37,177 --> 00:06:42,648
Outside the window, the stars crowd into a
blazing blue ring ahead, while the

87
00:06:42,648 --> 00:06:45,561
sky behind fades to a dull, red darkness.

88
00:06:48,888 --> 00:06:53,023
If a Starshot probe survives, it
gets exactly one chance.

89
00:06:53,323 --> 00:06:58,367
It cannot slow down. It will tear through
the system at about thirty-five

90
00:06:58,367 --> 00:07:01,062
times the Earth–Sun distance every day.

91
00:07:01,362 --> 00:07:06,396
In those few precious hours, its tiny camera
would take the first close-up

92
00:07:06,396 --> 00:07:08,573
pictures of another star system.

93
00:07:08,873 --> 00:07:13,767
Then a one-watt laser would send them home,
a whisper crossing four light

94
00:07:13,767 --> 00:07:14,974
years of darkness.

95
00:07:15,274 --> 00:07:19,050
And after that, the probe vanishes
into the dark, forever.

96
00:07:22,377 --> 00:07:25,662
So, is the journey to Alpha
Centauri possible?

97
00:07:25,962 --> 00:07:30,973
Not yet. Dust, radiation, and the sheer
distance still stand in the way.

98
00:07:31,273 --> 00:07:35,937
But the first steps are happening right now:
the lasers, the sails, and the

99
00:07:35,937 --> 00:07:37,865
telescopes hunting for planets.

100
00:07:38,165 --> 00:07:42,222
We are a species that once crossed
oceans in wooden boats.

101
00:07:42,522 --> 00:07:46,950
The ocean between the stars is wider,
and darker, and colder.

102
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:50,615
But the nearest shore is only
four light years away.

103
00:07:50,915 --> 00:07:55,808
If you enjoyed this journey, subscribe to
Technical Talk India, and tell us in

104
00:07:55,808 --> 00:07:58,317
the comments: would you board that ship?
